# Performance

Applies when **performance is a stated requirement or the change touches a
hot path**. Load for user-facing features, data pipelines, hot loops. Not
every chat — premature optimization is a real cost.

## Budget before optimization

- A performance change needs a budget: LCP / TBT / CLS for web, p95 / p99
  latency for APIs, throughput for pipelines. No budget → no target → no
  way to know when to stop.
- If the user gave no budget, ask once. If they don't know, propose a sane
  default and label it as a suggestion.

## Measure, then fix

- Measure before optimizing. A guess about where time goes is usually wrong.
- Fix the measured bottleneck, then re-measure. One bottleneck at a time.
- The cheapest fix is usually algorithmic (a better data structure, fewer
  passes, an index), not a micro-optimization.

## Hot paths

- N+1 queries, repeated parsing, re-reading the same file, synchronous I/O
  in a loop — these are the usual suspects. Check them first.
- Cache only what is expensive and stable. A cache invalidation bug is
  worse than no cache.

## Do not

- Optimize without a budget and a measurement.
- Trade correctness for speed without flagging it (a `ponytail:` comment
  naming the ceiling is fine).
- Add a dependency for a perf gain the stdlib already covers.
- Micro-optimize a path that isn't hot.
